 Partly, but I doubt that's the main reason. Two things probably matter more than missing transformations. I haven't checked any
  of this against your actual numbers.

  1. Chunks are probably being cut off before they're embedded. Chroma's default embedding model is all-MiniLM-L6-v2, and Chroma
  truncates its input to 256 tokens. Code splits into many tokens (identifiers, symbols, indentation), so a 2000-character chunk
  is roughly 500–800 tokens. If that's right, only the first third or so of most chunks ends up in the vector, while BM25 indexes
  every token. I'd check this first: it's cheap to measure, and the fix is easy (smaller chunks for dense, or a model that
  accepts longer input).

  2. The model doesn't fit the task. MiniLM is a small general model trained on English sentence pairs, not code. Your questions
  name exact identifiers (FP8_MIN, triton_flash_attention, "fused batched MoE"). BM25 is very good at that kind of exact match,
  and a small sentence model blurs those names into general meaning. BM25 beating dense on code questions is a known result;
  dense usually catches up on paraphrased, conceptual questions.

  Where transformations would help:
  - Contextual headers on chunks (file path, module, enclosing class/function added before embedding). This is the best fit for
    dense: a chunk from the middle of a function otherwise has no idea what it belongs to. It's a cheap first experiment.
  - HyDE / query rewriting. It closes the gap between a question and code by embedding a hypothetical answer, or code, instead of
    the question. It helps, but it costs an LLM call per query.
  - Identifier splitting (camelCase/snake_case). This mostly helps BM25; it matters less for dense, which has its own subword
    tokenizer.

  What I'd do, in order:
  1. Measure how many chunks go over 256 tokens. Then re-run dense with max_chunk_size around 600–800 and see how much recall
     comes back.
  2. Compare dense against BM25 on the docs dataset separately from code. If dense is close on docs but far behind on code, the
     model is the problem, not missing transformations.
  3. Try a stronger or code-aware embedding model. Since you already run Ollama, nomic-embed-text (8k context) is an easy switch
     through a custom Chroma embedding function.
  4. Then add contextual headers, then HyDE.

  Also, a dense retriever that's weaker on its own can still help hybrid. If hybrid beats BM25, dense is finding things BM25
  misses, even if it looks bad alone.

  I can run step 1 now; it only reads the corpus and doesn't touch your indexes.

